  "diagnosis_text": "The current path is h1\u2013s4\u2013s2\u2013s1\u2013s3\u2013s6\u2013h5, with all inter-switch links reported active. I\u2019ll measure connectivity and TCP throughput, then check for loss or queueing along that path.\nThe TCP test received only 0.197 Mbit/s, and ping averaged 158 ms. The strongest lead is s2\u2019s port 1 toward s1: it shows queued packets and repeated traffic-control overlimit events, unlike the other forward-path ports sampled. I\u2019ll compare transfers that stay on either side of that link and check whether the queueing persists.\n**The evidence localises the bottleneck to s2 port 1, transmitting toward s1 port 1**, on the h1\u2192h5 path:\n`h1 \u2192 s4 \u2192 s2 \u2192 s1 \u2192 s3 \u2192 s6 \u2192 h5`\n\nKey findings:\n- **h1\u2192h5:** only **0.197 Mbit/s** received; ping averaged **158 ms**, with 0/5 lost.\n- **s2\u2192s1:** two separate samples showed approximately **0.97\u20130.98 Mbit/s**, a **6,056-byte queue backlog**, and **255 queue overlimit events per two seconds**.\n- Other sampled forward-path ports had no queue backlog or overlimit events. No sampled port reported drops or errors.\n- Transfers bypassing this link were fast: **h1\u2192h3: 33.6 Gbit/s**, **h7\u2192h5: 33.4 Gbit/s** in this emulated network.\n- Reverse **h5\u2192h1** throughput was much higher, at **52.9 Mbit/s**, supporting a directional bottleneck.\n\n**Likely cause:** an egress traffic-control rate limit/shaper on **s2-eth1**, with ongoing traffic consuming its capacity. Traffic near 1 Mbit/s persisted outside the diagnostic throughput test. Overlimit events indicate shaping activity, not necessarily packet loss.\n\nNext, inspect the **qdisc/class configuration and statistics on s2-eth1**, including any parent rate limits, and identify traffic sharing that egress. The measurements strongly support shaping at that port, but do not establish the exact configured rate or the source of the ongoing traffic.",
